  "question": {
    "text": "A long straight wire with a circular cross-section having radius <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi></math>, is carrying a steady current <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>I</mi></math>. The current <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>I</mi></math> is uniformly distributed across this cross-section. Then the variation of magnetic field due to current <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>I</mi></math> with distance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>r</mi><mfenced><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#60;</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></mfenced></math> from its centre will be&nbsp;",
    "image": null
  },
