  "solution": {
    "text": "Using Ampere&#39;s law and noting that the steady current is uniformly distributed over the cross-section of the cylindrical wire.<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><mtable><mtr><mtd><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#956;</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>I</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#960;</mi><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>&#8804;</mo><mi>R</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#956;</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>I</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#960;</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>&#62;</mo><mi>R</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable></mfenced></math></p><p>Thus,&nbsp;magnetic field&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>B</mi><mo>&#8733;</mo><mi>r</mi></math>&nbsp;from the centre for <math><mi>r</mi><mo>&#60;</mo><mi>R</mi></math>.</p>",
